---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base title="Snapshot & pin · enprot">
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-3">Snapshot & pin</h1>
  <p class="text-enprot-muted mb-6">
    External verifiability without running a node. Pin the chain head hash,
    publish it out-of-band, anyone can verify against the pin.
  </p>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">Scenario</h2>
    <p>
      A vendor publishes a release. They want anyone — customers, security researchers,
      regulators — to be able to verify the release's history without running a full node
      or trusting the vendor's infrastructure. The vendor pins the chain head hash and
      publishes it through channels whose integrity is independently verifiable (their
      website over HTTPS, DNS TXT records, an anchoring service like OpenTimestamps that
      commits to the Bitcoin blockchain).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">Vendor: snapshot and publish</h2>
    <pre class="bg-enprot-ink text-enprot-paper p-4 rounded overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code># Build the release
enprot encrypt --signer release.pem -w ReleaseNotes release.ept

# Snapshot the chain head
enprot snapshot --publish print:// release.ept > published-hash.txt
# Output: &lt;head-hash&gt; &lt;timestamp&gt; &lt;signer-fp&gt;

# Publish the hash:
#   - On the vendor's website over HTTPS
#   - As a DNS TXT record
#   - Anchored in Bitcoin via OpenTimestamps
#   - In a tweet from the vendor's account
#   - On a printed invoice in the vendor's lobby
# The more independent publication channels, the stronger the pin.</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">Customer: verify against pin</h2>
    <pre class="bg-enprot-ink text-enprot-paper p-4 rounded overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code>curl -O https://vendor.example/release.ept
curl -O https://vendor.example/anchors/release-1.0.txt

enprot pin \
    --fetch https://vendor.example/anchors/release-1.0.txt \
    release.ept

# Or pin to a specific hash you obtained out of band:
enprot pin 7a4e9b… release.ept</code></pre>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm">
      If the file's current chain head doesn't match the pinned hash, verification fails.
      The vendor cannot retroactively alter history without breaking the pin.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">Pluggable anchoring services</h2>
    <pre class="bg-enprot-ink text-enprot-paper p-4 rounded overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code>trait AnchorService &#123;
    fn publish(&self, head: Hash) -> Result&lt;PublicationRef&gt;;
    fn fetch(&self, r: PublicationRef) -> Result&lt;(Hash, Timestamp, Signature)&gt;;
&#125;</code></pre>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm">
      Built-in: <code>file://</code> (local), <code>print://</code> (manual). Future:
      <code>opentimestamps://</code>, <code>dns://</code>, internal corporate services.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">Capability framing</h2>
    <p>
      The pin itself is just a hash — anyone can verify it. The vendor's capability is
      <code>Signer(release_fp)</code> at snapshot time; verifiers hold
      <code>Verifier(release_fp)</code> and <code>Verifier(tsa_fp)</code> if using a TSA.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">Status</h2>
    <p>
      Specified in <a href="https://github.com/engyon/enprot/blob/main/TODO.finalize/30-snapshot-pin.md" class="text-enprot-accent hover:underline"><code>TODO.finalize/30</code></a>.
      Depends on Stage 1 (chain head hash is the chain's current tip).
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
